<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<!--
  The default video surface: a SurfaceView.

  `surface_type` has to be set here rather than in code — PlayerView only reads
  it from an XML AttributeSet at construction time, so a plain
  `PlayerView(context)` call, which is what a Compose `AndroidView` factory
  would otherwise use, can never select it. That is the only reason this file
  exists. See player_view_texture.xml for the compatibility variant.

  Why SurfaceView is the default: it is given its own hardware overlay plane,
  so decoded frames are handed to the display controller directly and the GPU
  never composites them. A TextureView is an ordinary texture inside the view
  hierarchy, so every single frame is a GPU composite pass — on a low-end box
  that is measurable heat, power, and dropped frames at 1080p60, and it was
  visible in this app's own profiling as tens of megabytes of extra GPU memory.

  This app briefly defaulted to a TextureView while chasing a black-picture-
  with-working-audio report. That turned out to be an app bug — the player was
  never attached to the surface at all — so the TextureView was paying a real
  cost to work around something it never fixed. It remains available behind
  Settings → "Compatibility video surface" for the genuine firmware cases,
  where a vendor display stack composites the overlay plane incorrectly
  underneath a Compose UI.
-->
<androidx.media3.ui.PlayerView
    xmlns:android="http://schemas.android.com/apk/res/android"
    xmlns:app="http://schemas.android.com/apk/res-auto"
    android:id="@+id/player_view"
    android:layout_width="match_parent"
    android:layout_height="match_parent"
    android:background="@android:color/black"
    app:surface_type="surface_view"
    app:use_controller="false" />
